[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"646","stub":{"id":"646","section":"2. Colt Killed Creek Trailhead to Lochsa River","altname":"","river":"Colt Killed Creek (White Sand Creek)","difficulty":"IIItoIV","states":["USA-IDA"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":null,"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"The Forest Service Colt Creek Trailhead is located where Forest Road 359 terminates at the river. From the take-out bridge, head left towards Elk Summit on Forest Road 111. In 5.4 miles from Highway 12 you will reach a junction where you will turn right onto Forest Road 360 continuing on up towards Elk Summit. At mile 8.4 you will reach Savage Pass, the highest point on the shuttle drive at 6168' (this may be snowed in through late June in heavy snow years). At this point you will be headed back downhill and at 11.1 miles from Highway 12 you will reach the turn off for Forest Road 359 which is the road to the Colt Creek Trailhead. Turn left onto this road and wind your way 5.4 miles down to the river. The road ends at the trail bridge crossing White Sand Creek which is the put-in. Forest Road 359 is steep and prone to washouts so you may not be able to drive all the way down to the river. When you can it typically requires a high-clearance vehicle. Another option, popular with packrafters, is to skip the drive and hike up Forest Service trail 50 that parallels river right the whole way.","id":"111210","location":{"latitude":"46.4335088","longitude":"-114.54061745"},"name":"Colt Creek Trailhead Put-In","distance":"0","photoID":"883833","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"other","description":"The confluence where Storm Creek joins Colt Killed Creek. The rapids transition here and ease up a bit. For packrafters hiking up on Forest Service Trail 50 the approximately 10 miles from the take-out, this is a good starting point or you can continue on up the trail for more challenging whitewater.","id":"wGfh5c7XMY9xsm0h3qMtQ","location":{"latitude":"46.4633131","longitude":"-114.54920666"},"name":"Storm Creek Confluence","distance":"2.47","photoID":"883831","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":"The take out at the bridge over Colt Killed Creek is just upstream of the start of the Lochsa River. From Highway 12 mile 163.5 turn down the Forest Service road to White Sand Campground. It's 1.2 miles down to the bridges across the Crooked Fork and Colt Killed Creek which join to form the Lochsa.You can also continue a short distance on the Lochsa to White Sand Campground and easier low bank access.","id":"116478","location":{"latitude":"46.50906897","longitude":"-114.68087061"},"name":"Colt Killed Creek Bridge Take-Out","distance":"12.35","photoID":"XzgxAiKVUi2F7q9iB8lg8","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"883830","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886560","description":"<p>One of many fun drops on the upper section of White Sands Creek. At 7800 cfs on the Lochsa gage.</p>","caption":"White Sand Creek","poi_name":"","subject":"Steve Corsi","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2014-06-23T15:54:40.000Z","updated_at":"2026-04-02T15:54:40.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883830","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883830"}},{"id":"883834","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886560","description":"<p>Driving in over Savage Pass (6168') for an early season run.</p>","caption":"Savage Pass","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2014-06-23T15:54:40.000Z","updated_at":"2026-04-02T15:54:40.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883834","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883834"}},{"id":"883828","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886560","description":"<p>Lining up for the finale on the first big rapid around the corner from the put-in. At 7800 cfs on the Lochsa gage.</p>","caption":"First big drop on White Sand","poi_name":"","subject":"Omar Jepperson","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2014-06-23T15:54:40.000Z","updated_at":"2026-04-02T15:54:40.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883828","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883828"}},{"id":"883836","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886560","description":"<p>The run starts off immediately below the trail bridge with fun boogie water (at 7800 cfs on the Lochsa gage).</p>","caption":"Start of the Run","poi_name":"","subject":"Paul Janda","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2014-06-23T15:54:40.000Z","updated_at":"2026-04-02T15:54:40.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883836","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883836"}},{"id":"883829","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886560","description":"<p>One of many fun drops on the upper section of White Sands Creek. At 7800 cfs on the Lochsa gage.</p>","caption":"White Sand Creek","poi_name":"","subject":"Paul Janda","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2014-06-23T15:54:40.000Z","updated_at":"2026-04-02T15:54:40.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883829","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883829"}},{"id":"883835","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886560","description":"","caption":"Middle Section","poi_name":"","subject":"Morgan Giddings","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2014-06-23T15:54:40.000Z","updated_at":"2026-04-02T15:54:40.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883835","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883835"}},{"id":"883831","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886560","description":"<p>Storm Creek confluence with White Sands Creek.</p>","caption":"Storm Creek Pack Bridge","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2014-06-23T15:54:40.000Z","updated_at":"2026-04-02T15:54:40.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883831","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883831"}},{"id":"883833","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886560","description":"<p>Put-in at the Colt Killed Creek Trailhead bridge.</p>","caption":"Put-in","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2014-06-23T15:54:40.000Z","updated_at":"2026-04-02T15:54:40.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883833","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883833"}},{"id":"883832","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886560","description":"<p>The last class II rapids at the take-out.</p>","caption":"Final rapids","poi_name":"","subject":"Morgan Giddings","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2014-06-23T15:54:40.000Z","updated_at":"2026-04-02T15:54:40.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883832","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883832"}},{"id":"XzgxAiKVUi2F7q9iB8lg8","ext":"jpg","post_id":"CDqGenQTTOy3lyf278LIe","description":"","caption":"Colt Killed Creek Bridge Take-Out","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":"116478","photo_date":"2022-06-20T16:11:23.000Z","updated_at":"2026-04-02T16:11:24.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/XzgxAiKVUi2F7q9iB8lg8","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/XzgxAiKVUi2F7q9iB8lg8"}},{"id":"6DG1a1GSgNDGGDD2eOgC5","ext":"jpg","post_id":"CDqGenQTTOy3lyf278LIe","description":"Downstream View of Colt Killed Creek where it joins the Crooked Fork to become the Lochsa.","caption":"Start of the Lochca","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":"116478","photo_date":"2022-06-20T16:11:23.000Z","updated_at":"2026-04-02T16:11:24.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6DG1a1GSgNDGGDD2eOgC5","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6DG1a1GSgNDGGDD2eOgC5"}},{"id":"aJbPb5q89RRJ0knu96TOP","ext":"jpg","post_id":"CDqGenQTTOy3lyf278LIe","description":"Downstream river right bank at Colt Killed Creek Bridge.","caption":"Colt Killed Creek Bridge Take-Out","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":"116478","photo_date":"2022-06-20T16:11:23.000Z","updated_at":"2026-04-02T16:11:24.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/aJbPb5q89RRJ0knu96TOP","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/aJbPb5q89RRJ0knu96TOP"}},{"id":"11414","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6743","description":"","caption":"un rapido...","poi_name":"???","subject":"Jon Crane","author":"Brian Mattingly","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/11414","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/11414"}},{"id":"39902","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3426","description":"White Sands Creek is a beautiful river / creek flowing through a Roadless Area.   ","caption":"An Idaho Roadless Gem","poi_name":"Put In","subject":"","author":"Kevin Colburn","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/39902","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/39902"}},{"id":"11411","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6743","description":"This is part of the hike back to the putin from storm creek. ","caption":"the hike out...","poi_name":"","subject":"Jon Crane","author":"Brian Mattingly","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/11411","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/11411"}},{"id":"11413","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6743","description":"rain.","caption":"whitesands...","poi_name":"","subject":"...","author":"Brian Mattingly","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/11413","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/11413"}},{"id":"39903","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3426","description":"This photo was taken looking downstream from the put in. ","caption":"White Sand Put In","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Kevin Colburn","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/39903","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/39903"}},{"id":"11412","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6743","description":"This is a pretty much manditory portage we came across. You might be able to limbo it but it's an easy walk. ","caption":"portage...","poi_name":"???","subject":"Jon Crane","author":"Brian Mattingly","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/11412","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/11412"}}],"detail":{"id":"646","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"Lower Colt Killed Creek is a great run for people who seek a long, remote, and beautiful day on a small river without dealing with super hard rapids. The rapids start with class IV, tapering off to class III, and finally class II. Without any big/steep drops, some paddlers take their playboats for this run. It is generally runnable whenever the Lochsa has a medium flow in it.\n\nThis creek flows through a large Forest Service roadless area managed as backcountry which means that removal of timber is generally not allowed and new roads will not be constructed. This creek flows through a remarkably beautiful and wild place. The water quality is superb, and paddlers have seen lots of wildlife on this run including cougars.\n\nFrom the trail bridge at the put-in the action starts right away as you head down a long straightaway and then reach the first and biggest rapid on the run. This drop has a blind corner as the river takes a sweeping turn to the left and then back to the right behind a big rock and finishing off with a ledge that is not visible from upstream. You can get a good view by committing to the first part of the drop and grabbing an eddy near the top on river left where you can hop out for a quick scout. Wood can be an issue on this run especially in the first couple miles.\n\nFrom this point on, most all of the drops can be easily boat scouted and you will encounter plenty of great continous class IV rapids all the way to Storm Creek. Once you pass Storm Creek the whitewater transitions to more class III/IV. The whitewater is still continuous but drops are a bit more spaced out and the increased flow provides a more open feel with more options.\n\nAfter a couple miles the river backs off to fun class III--but keep an eye out for boat-grabbing holes--and once you are into the class II you will know that you are nearing the take-out where the river joins the Crooked Fork to become the Lochsa.\n\nLogistics: This is a long 16 mile shuttle on Forest Service roads up and over the 6168' high Savage Pass and back down into the river corridor. It is helpful to have a shuttle driver. If you do not have a shuttle available, some have done the first couple miles of the run, that includes the best section of class IV whitewater, and hiked back to the put-in from the Storm Creek confluence. Another atlernative that some packrafters have been doing is to hike up the Colt Killed Creek Trail (Forest Service Trail 50) from the take-out at the Lochsa confluence to Storm Creek or above. The higher you hike up this creek, the more challenging the rapids.","averageGradient":null,"maxGradient":null,"length":"12.4","correlations":[],"pointOfInterestIDs":["111210","wGfh5c7XMY9xsm0h3qMtQ","116478"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-114.54011693,46.43353413],[-114.54011693,46.43378213],[-114.54011693,46.43400339],[-114.54016273,46.43439253],